10:46:02 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: The stomach does not usually treat food as separate, neatly timed “batches.” Instead, it acts more like a mixing and metering chamber. - **New food enters the upper stomach**, which can relax and serve as a temporary reservoir. - **Muscular contractions mix and grind the contents**, especially in the lower stomach (the antrum), combining newer and older food into a semi-liquid mixture called *chyme*. - The **pyloric sphincter** at the stomach’s exit opens only a little at a time. With each contraction, small amounts of sufficiently liquefied material are pushed into the small intestine; larger particles are pushed back for more grinding. So there usually is not a strict “older food leaves first, newer food stays behind” system. There can be some layering and partial ordering—liquids often leave faster than solids, for example—but the contents become substantially mixed. Eating every 30 minutes generally means the stomach may stay partly full and continue emptying continuously rather than completely emptying between meals. The rate of emptying also changes based on what was eaten: - **Liquids and carbohydrate-rich foods** tend to leave relatively quickly. - **Protein** is slower. - **Fat, fiber, and large solid particles** tend to slow emptying. The “1–2 hours” figure is only a rough estimate and often applies to certain meal types; a mixed or high-fat meal may take several hours to empty substantially.
