10:46:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It can feel that way, and there is some real physical basis for it—but the stomach is not identifying food by its “age” the way a sorting system would. After a large meal: - The **older, heavier solid meal** is often being held and ground mainly in the lower stomach. - A later snack—especially if it is a **drink, soft food, sugar/carbohydrate, or small amount of food**—may mix with stomach contents differently and can empty relatively quickly. - The upper stomach can accommodate newly eaten food, while the lower stomach selectively grinds solids and lets only small enough particles through the pylorus. So a newly eaten liquid or easily digested snack may indeed leave the stomach sooner than parts of an earlier, fatty or solid lunch. That is mostly due to **physical properties**—liquid vs. solid, particle size, calories, fat content, and volume—not because the stomach knows which was eaten first. Also, the feeling that something “passed through” soon after eating does not necessarily mean that exact new food has reached the intestines or exited the body. Eating can trigger the **gastrocolic reflex**, which stimulates colon movement and can create urges, gurgling, or a sense of movement. Often that is older material already in the intestine/colon being moved along. A lingering heavy/full feeling after a large meal is common, especially with high-fat meals, large portions, alcohol, or eating quickly. But frequent severe fullness, nausea, vomiting, pain, unintended weight loss, or feeling full after very little food can warrant medical evaluation for delayed gastric emptying or other digestive conditions.
