10:46:29 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your teacher’s “first in, first out” description is a useful classroom simplification, and it is often **roughly true for a mixed solid meal**: food eaten earlier tends to have had more time to be ground into small particles and therefore is more likely to empty sooner. But it is not a strict queue. The stomach has regions with different jobs: - **Upper stomach (fundus/body):** receives and stores newly swallowed food. - **Lower stomach (antrum):** vigorously mixes/grinds food and pushes small particles toward the outlet. - **Pylorus:** lets mostly liquid and very small particles enter the duodenum in small pulses. Because food commonly enters at the top while emptying occurs from the lower stomach, there can be a degree of age-related organization: earlier food may be farther along in the grinding/emptying process. That is likely what your teacher meant. However, stomach contents also mix, and emptying depends strongly on food properties. A later glass of water, juice, or a soft low-fat snack can pass through the stomach faster than a portion of an earlier high-fat, high-fiber, or solid meal. Conversely, adding more food can alter stomach motility and slow overall emptying. So the most accurate version is: > The stomach often has a *general tendency* for earlier solid food to progress toward emptying first, but it does not maintain a strict first-in-first-out separation. It mixes, sorts by particle size and consistency, and releases contents gradually.
