10:46:45 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It’s not random chaos—but it also does not require the stomach to “know” the age of each piece of food. Think of it more like a **mixing tank with an outlet at the bottom**, not a set of labeled containers. Order emerges from the physical setup: 1. **New food arrives mostly into the upper stomach.** 2. **Older contents have often already moved farther toward the lower stomach**, where contractions are strongest. 3. The lower stomach repeatedly **grinds and mixes** contents. 4. The outlet lets out mainly **liquid and very small particles** in controlled pulses. That naturally gives a *general* first-in-first-out tendency for solids: food that has been in the stomach longer has had more chances to be mixed, broken up, and moved toward the exit. But “general tendency” is not “strict sequence.” A later liquid, for example, may travel through channels between solid material and empty quickly. A later soft snack may be processed faster than an earlier steak-and-fries meal. Fat in the small intestine can also signal the stomach to slow emptying, affecting both old and new contents. The stages of digestion are predictable because the stomach and intestine regulate **what physical form** can pass, **how quickly** it passes, and **how much** the small intestine can handle—not because the stomach tracks each item by the time it was eaten. So your teacher’s version captures the usual big-picture pattern: earlier solid food often progresses first. The more precise physiology is that this pattern comes from location, mixing, particle size, meal composition, and coordinated muscle activity—not a perfect “first bite in, first bite out” system.
